From: Asahi Linux contributors <asahi@lists.linux.dev>
Subject: [PATCH] fairydust: USB-C DisplayPort alt mode (14 commits)
X-Summary: USB-C DisplayPort output (the fairydust work)
X-Who-Needs-It: Only needed if your kernel is built from the release branch. Redundant on the fairydust branch, where it is detected and skipped.

Squashed range asahi-7.1.5-2..fairydust from AsahiLinux/linux, which is
exactly the delta between the release branch and the fairydust branch:
14 commits, 0 behind.

  e2566051 fixup! arm64: configs: asahi: Add new configs for v7.1
  19f8a052 usb: typec: tipd: Track data_status changes for CD321x
  ecb9073f usb: typec: tipd: HACK: Use drm oob hotplug event
  e603264b arm64: dts: apple: t8103: Add dp-altmode dts hacks
  1022ad9c HACK: arm64: dts: apple: t8103: Mark ps_atc1_common as always on
  94158a16 arm64: dts: apple: t8112: Add dp-altmode dts hacks
  31186a1d HACK: arm64: dts: apple: t8112: Mark ps_atc1_common as always on
  05057727 arm64: apple: t8112-j473: Add DP-altmode on typec1
  ba81d86f HACK: arm64: apple: t8112-j473: Mark ps_atc1_common as always on
  e4bd0f15 arm64: dts: apple: t60xx: j[34]1[46]: Add dp-altmode hacks
  449d9961 HACK: arm64: dts: apple: t60xx: j[34]1[46]: Mark ps_atc1_common as always on
  352b8830 arm64: dts: apple: t60xx: j[34]75: Add dp-altmode hacks
  30a5013c HACK: arm64: dts: apple: t60xx: j[34]75: Mark ps_atc1_common as always on
  e3e35907 arm64: dts: apple: t6020-j474s: Add dp-altmode hacks

This exists so distributions that build the release branch, such as Asahi
ALARM's linux-asahi, can get USB-C DisplayPort output without switching
their kernel source to a different branch.

The first commit in that list is not USB-C work. It is an arm64 config
fixup that sits on fairydust but not on the release tag, and it comes
along because this is a plain range diff rather than a curated selection.
It touches arch/arm64/configs/asahi.config only.

Upstream marks several of these commits HACK, and the fairydust branch is
explicitly experimental. In particular the ps_atc1_common power domain is
forced always-on, which has battery implications on a laptop. Only one
USB-C port drives a display.

Not authored here. Provenance:
https://github.com/AsahiLinux/linux/compare/asahi-7.1.5-2...fairydust
---
